= Genetics =

== Overview ==
'''Genetics''' is the branch of biology concerned with heredity, genes, genetic variation, and how inherited information contributes to biological traits.

Genetics studies how biological information is transmitted and varied across generations and cells. It includes inheritance patterns, gene function, mutation, recombination, genotype–phenotype relationships, and interactions between genetic variation and environment.

Modern genetics spans molecular, population, quantitative, developmental, and medical approaches. A gene can influence a trait without being its sole cause; many traits arise from multiple genetic and environmental factors.

Genetics overlaps with genomics but is not identical to it. Genomics emphasizes genome-scale organization and interaction across many genes and other genomic elements.

== Boundaries ==
* genetics ≠ genomics
* gene influence ≠ single-gene determinism
* heritability ≠ immutability

== Related concepts ==
[[Genomics]], [[Biological sequence]], [[Phylogenetics]], [[Causality]], [[Probability]], and [[Measurement]] provide neighboring concepts.

== Mechanisms of inherited variation ==
Inheritance of DNA sequence variants is a set of molecular and organismal processes, rather than a guarantee that each characteristic will be reproduced unchanged. Chromosome segregation, recombination and mutation affect the combinations that descendants receive. [[Related to::Genetic inheritance|Genetic inheritance]] addresses these mechanisms explicitly, including distinctions between sexual reproduction and horizontal genetic transfer in microbes.

== Genotype, phenotype and environmental context ==
A '''genotype''' describes genetic variant state at a locus or set of loci. A '''phenotype''' is an observable or measurable characteristic under stated conditions. The mapping between them can involve gene regulation, developmental history, interactions among loci, and environment. An association between a variant and trait is not automatically a causal mechanism, and a measured heritability estimate is population- and environment-dependent rather than an individual's fixed percentage of genetic determination.

== Evidence and experimental design ==
Family and segregation studies, crosses, genomic sequencing, population association studies and intervention experiments answer different questions. Co-segregation and association help localize candidate variants; functional experiments can test mechanisms, but experimental systems may not generalize to every population or environment. [[Related to::Genomics|Genomics]] extends many such questions to genome-wide organization and interactions.

